# Custom Dynamic MOTD for Debian 13

This guide shows you how to replace the static `/etc/motd` with a dynamic, eye-catching message that updates on each login. We’ll leverage Debian’s `pam_motd` and the `/etc/update-motd.d` directory to break the MOTD into reusable, version-controlled scripts—so each piece can be tweaked independently.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

- A Debian 13 server with root or sudo access  
- `bash`, `awk`, `sed`, and coreutils installed (default on Debian)  
- Optional: `jq` (for JSON), `kubectl` (if you want cluster info)  

---

## 2. Disable the Static `/etc/motd`

1. Move away the old MOTD so PAM won’t print it:
   ```bash
   sudo mv /etc/motd /etc/motd.static
   ```
2. Ensure `/etc/pam.d/sshd` (and other services) include:
   ```text
   session    optional   pam_motd.so  motd=/run/motd.dynamic
   ```
   Debian’s defaults already point at `/run/motd.dynamic`.

---

## 3. Create the `/etc/update-motd.d/` Directory

If it doesn’t exist, create it and clear out old scripts:
```bash
sudo mkdir -p /etc/update-motd.d
sudo chmod 755 /etc/update-motd.d
sudo rm -f /etc/update-motd.d/*
```

Every executable script in this folder runs in numeric order; its stdout is concatenated into `/run/motd.dynamic`.

---

## 4. Script: 00-header

Print a banner with hostname, date, and a touch of color.
```bash
sudo tee /etc/update-motd.d/00-header > /dev/null <<'EOF'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ASCII banner + hostname + date

ESC=$(printf "\033")
RESET="${ESC}[0m"
BOLD="${ESC}[1m"
CYAN="${ESC}[36m"

cat <<EOB

${CYAN}${BOLD}
   ____             _     ____  _   _ 
  |  _ \  ___   ___| | __/ ___|| |_| |
  | | | |/ _ \ / __| |/ /\___ \| __| |
  | |_| | (_) | (__|   <  ___) | |_|_|
  |____/ \___/ \___|_|\_\|____/ \__(_)
${RESET}

 System: ${BOLD}$(hostname -f)${RESET}    Date: ${BOLD}$(date +"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")${RESET}

EOB
EOF
sudo chmod +x /etc/update-motd.d/00-header
```

